"level": "AMC 8",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 4,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "The twelve-sided figure shown has been drawn on \\(1 \\text{ cm}\\times 1 \\text{ cm}\\) graph paper. What is the area of the figure in \\(\\text{cm}^2?\\)\n\n[Figure 1](figures/2018_AMC_8_Q04_question_fig1.svg)",
"questionType": "mixed",
"choices": {
"A": "\\( 12 \\)",
"B": "\\( 12.5 \\)",
"C": "\\( 13 \\)",
"D": "\\( 13.5 \\)",
"E": "\\( 14 \\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "C",
"solution": "The slanted edges make the outline awkward to count directly. Draw the dashed segments shown below to separate a central square from four congruent triangular points.\n\n[Figure 1](figures/2018_AMC_8_Q04_official_expansion_solution_fig1.png)\n\nThe central square \\(RSTU\\) is \\(3\\) grid units on each side, so its area is \\(3\\cdot3=9\\). The top triangle \\(REF\\) has a horizontal base \\(RF\\) of length \\(2\\) and a perpendicular height of \\(1\\), giving area \\(\\frac12\\cdot2\\cdot1=1\\). Each of the other three triangles has the same dimensions.\n\nThese five pieces fill the figure without overlap, so its area is \\(9+4\\cdot1=\\boxed{13}\\text{ cm}^2\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\). A grid outline can become easier to measure by separating a simple central region from its points.",
"solutionSource": "Adapted from MAA official solutions",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://drive.usercontent.google.com/uc?id=1RcfdsJkYkLvanhwRU9GWkLBt-2OlMOA0&export=download",
