"year": 2018,
"level": "AMC 8",
"batch": "NA",
"number": 21,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "How many positive three-digit integers have a remainder of \\(2\\) when divided by \\(6,\\) a remainder of \\(5\\) when divided by \\(9,\\) and a remainder of \\(7\\) when divided by \\(11?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\( 1 \\)",
"B": "\\( 2 \\)",
"C": "\\( 3 \\)",
"D": "\\( 4 \\)",
"E": "\\( 5 \\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "E",
"solution": "Each stated remainder is four less than its divisor. Thus if the integer is \\(n\\), adding \\(4\\) makes it divisible by \\(6\\), \\(9\\), and \\(11\\) all at once.\n\nTheir least common multiple is \\(2\\cdot3^2\\cdot11=198\\), so \\(n+4=198k\\) for an integer \\(k\\). Conversely, subtracting \\(4\\) from any multiple of \\(198\\) gives the required remainders \\(2,5,7\\), so this description includes exactly the solutions.\n\nThe three-digit condition gives\n\n\\[\n100\\leq198k-4\\leq999,\n\\]\n\nor \\(104\\leq198k\\leq1003\\). Hence \\(k=1,2,3,4,5\\), producing\n\n\\[\n194,392,590,788,986.\n\\]\n\nThere are \\(\\boxed{5}\\) integers, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\). A common shortfall from each divisor suggests adding that amount before taking a least common multiple.",
"solutionSource": "Adapted from MAA official solutions",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://drive.usercontent.google.com/uc?id=1RcfdsJkYkLvanhwRU9GWkLBt-2OlMOA0&export=download",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc8/2018/solutions",
