"number": 4,
"area": "Unclassified",
"question": "Quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\) is a rhombus with perimeter \\(52\\) meters. The length of diagonal \\(\\overline{AC}\\) is \\(24\\) meters. What is the area in square meters of rhombus \\(ABCD?\\)\n\n[Figure 1](figures/2019_AMC_8_Q04_question_fig1.svg)",
"questionType": "mixed",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(60\\)",
"B": "\\(90\\)",
"C": "\\(105\\)",
"D": "\\(120\\)",
"E": "\\(144\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "Draw the other diagonal \\(BD\\), and call its intersection with \\(AC\\) point \\(M\\). The diagonals of a rhombus bisect one another at right angles, producing four congruent right triangles.\n\n[Figure 1](figures/2019_AMC_8_Q04_official_expansion_solution_fig1.png)\n\nEach side of the rhombus is \\(52/4=13\\) meters, and \\(AM=24/2=12\\) meters. In right triangle \\(ABM\\), the side \\(AB\\) is the hypotenuse. The Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n\\[\nBM^2=13^2-12^2=169-144=25,\n\\]\n\nso \\(BM=5\\) meters. Each of the four right triangles has area \\(\\frac12\\cdot12\\cdot5=30\\) square meters. Thus the rhombus has area \\(4\\cdot30=\\boxed{120}\\) square meters, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).\n\nThe diagonals are useful because they turn the given side and diagonal lengths into a right-triangle calculation.",
"solutionSource": "Adapted from MAA official solutions",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://drive.usercontent.google.com/uc?id=1LNwtmi2U96Fqq23aquyLcXk96BpgcqF4&export=download",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc8/2019/solutions",
