"level": "AMC 10",
"batch": "A",
"number": 8,
"area": "Prealgebra",
"question": "A data set consists of \\(6\\) (not distinct) positive integers: \\(1,\\) \\(7,\\) \\(5,\\) \\(2,\\) \\(5,\\) and \\(X.\\) The average (arithmetic mean) of the \\(6\\) numbers equals a value in the data set. What is the sum of all possible values of \\(X?\\)",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(10\\)",
"B": "\\(26\\)",
"C": "\\(32\\)",
"D": "\\(36\\)",
"E": "\\(40\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "The five known entries add to \\(20\\), so the mean is \\((20+X)/6\\). It must equal one of \\(1,2,5,7,X\\).\n\nBecause \\(X\\ge1\\), the mean is at least \\(21/6=3.5\\). This rules out \\(1\\) and \\(2\\). The remaining cases give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(20+X)/6=5&\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad X=10,\\\\\n(20+X)/6=7&\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad X=22,\\\\\n(20+X)/6=X&\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad X=4.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nAll three values are positive integers and make the mean a listed entry. Their sum is \\(4+10+22=\\boxed{36}\\), choice D.\n\nWhen a mean must be a member of its own data set, list the possible mean values before solving.",
"solutionSource": "Adapted from MAA official solutions",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://maa.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/2022-AMC-10A-solutions.pdf",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc10/2022A/solutions",
