"number": 14,
"area": "Counting & Probability",
"question": "In how many ways can the letters in BEEKEEPER be rearranged so that two or more E’s do not appear together?",
"questionType": "text",
"choices": {
"A": "\\(1\\)",
"B": "\\(4\\)",
"C": "\\(12\\)",
"D": "\\(24\\)",
"E": "\\(120\\)"
},
"choicesType": "text",
"answer": "D",
"solution": "There are five E's and only four other letters: B, K, P, and R. To keep the E's apart, each of the four gaps between consecutive E's needs one of those other letters. This uses every non-E letter, so the pattern is forced:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm E\\ \\_\\ \\mathrm E\\ \\_\\ \\mathrm E\\ \\_\\ \\mathrm E\\ \\_\\ \\mathrm E.\n\\]\n\nOnly the order of B, K, P, and R remains to be chosen. There are \\(4\\) choices for the first blank, then \\(3\\), then \\(2\\), then \\(1\\), giving\n\n\\[\n4\\cdot3\\cdot2\\cdot1=\\boxed{24}\n\\]\n\narrangements, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\). The identical E's do not create additional arrangements.",
"solutionSource": "Adapted from MAA official solutions",
"solutionSourceURL": "https://drive.usercontent.google.com/uc?id=1h0XYnHhGAtM4i3JxvGUgozPrlhZiYZQb&export=download",
"sourceURL": "https://live.poshenloh.com/past-contests/amc8/2022/solutions",
"figureSources": {},
"editorialNote": "",
